1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:05,442
Finish wiring your schematic, then choose Breadboard, or Build my breadboard in the lab.

2
00:00:05,642 --> 00:00:08,309
Your parts are available for you to place and connect.

3
00:00:08,509 --> 00:00:10,045
Keep Power off while building.

4
00:00:10,245 --> 00:00:14,149
The completed board shown here is a reference for the final arrangement.

5
00:00:15,049 --> 00:00:18,825
In Parts, choose This circuit to see components matching your schematic.

6
00:00:19,025 --> 00:00:21,862
The wider Parts kit contains extra components.

7
00:00:22,062 --> 00:00:24,153
Choose a part and place it on the board.

8
00:00:24,353 --> 00:00:28,918
Use the schematic values and references to keep the two versions consistent.

9
00:00:29,818 --> 00:00:34,298
Breadboard holes in each five-hole terminal strip are electrically connected.

10
00:00:34,498 --> 00:00:37,293
The center gap separates the upper and lower strips.

11
00:00:37,493 --> 00:00:40,479
Place a resistor across different electrical nodes.

12
00:00:40,679 --> 00:00:44,285
If both leads are in the same strip, the resistor is shorted out.

13
00:00:45,185 --> 00:00:48,726
Guide me identifies the next component and its target holes.

14
00:00:48,926 --> 00:00:52,958
Read the explanation, find the matching part, and place it yourself.

15
00:00:53,158 --> 00:00:55,270
Show placement previews the location.

16
00:00:55,470 --> 00:01:00,771
Do this step performs a placement for you, so record that assistance when your instructor requires

17
00:01:00,771 --> 00:01:01,102
it.

18
00:01:02,002 --> 00:01:06,738
For the MOSFET build, verify which virtual leads are gate, drain, and source.

19
00:01:06,938 --> 00:01:15,066
A real ZVN3310F uses an adapter, so trace its actual header mapping before wiring hardware.

20
00:01:15,266 --> 00:01:19,725
Do not assume the generic virtual package has the same physical lead order.

21
00:01:20,625 --> 00:01:23,334
Use Jumper wire to connect the required holes.

22
00:01:23,534 --> 00:01:27,993
Work one schematic node at a time, rather than copying a wire color alone.

23
00:01:28,193 --> 00:01:34,626
For CS, connect the positive and negative supplies correctly and keep zero-volt ground separate
from

24
00:01:34,626 --> 00:01:36,342
the minus fifteen-volt rail.

25
00:01:37,242 --> 00:01:40,911
Open Check or Doctor and inspect each reported connection.

26
00:01:41,111 --> 00:01:45,186
Correct missing jumpers, wrong holes, and incorrect terminal mappings.

27
00:01:45,386 --> 00:01:49,525
The green check shown here means this reference board matches its schematic.

28
00:01:49,725 --> 00:01:53,458
It does not prove that the schematic itself meets the lab targets.

29
00:01:54,358 --> 00:02:00,246
Connect CH1 to the input and CH2 to the drain for CS, or the collector for CE.

30
00:02:00,446 --> 00:02:04,499
Connect both scope grounds and generator common to zero volts.

31
00:02:04,699 --> 00:02:08,539
After the wiring check, switch power on and inspect the measurements.

32
00:02:08,739 --> 00:02:10,830
Power off before changing connections.

33
00:02:11,730 --> 00:02:14,546
Save the circuit project and your progress backup.

34
00:02:14,746 --> 00:02:19,930
Capture your own completed breadboard, and record any fault you corrected or help you used.

35
00:02:20,130 --> 00:02:24,034
Add the image and notes to the matching CS or CE report.

36
00:02:24,234 --> 00:02:28,863
Virtual measurements and physical hardware measurements belong in separate columns.
